#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fpms-calibrate-lidar - measure base_link -> laser_frame. Never moves the rover.

The operator pushes and turns the rover BY HAND. This program only WATCHES:
it subscribes to /scan_lidar and /imu, and it estimates the LiDAR mount
transform from the way the world appears to move.

============================================================================
THIS PROGRAM PUBLISHES NOTHING. NOT TO ANY TOPIC, AT ALL.
============================================================================
There is no create_publisher call anywhere in this file - not /cmd_vel, not
/cmd_duty, not even a diagnostic topic. That is a hard requirement, not a
preference, and it buys three things:

  * there is no safety surface. Nothing here can move the rover, so this needs
    no arming, no stop authority and no motion calibration;
  * every estimate below depends on the DIRECTION of apparent motion and never
    on its magnitude, so counts/mm being unmeasured and disputed does not
    matter to any number this program produces;
  * the gyro is an independent witness, because heading comes from integrating
    angular_velocity.z rather than from the wheels.

The node self-checks this after it starts (see Listener._assert_no_publishers)
rather than relying on the claim in this comment. Never trust a promise where
you can verify an outcome.

============================================================================
WHAT IS BEING MEASURED, AND WHY IT IS WORTH AN EVENING
============================================================================
`base_link -> laser_frame` in nav2/fpms_tf.launch.py is six MEASURE ME
placeholders, and /etc/fpms/config.env ships all six as zeros with
FPMS_TF_OFFSETS_MEASURED=0. By the launch file's own arithmetic:

    1 degree of mount yaw  ~=  10.5 mm of position error

and BOTH the obstacle cone guard AND the occupancy grid inherit that error in
the SAME direction, so it accumulates over a mission instead of averaging out.
fpms-nav2.service and both SLAM units refuse to start until
FPMS_TF_OFFSETS_MEASURED=1, which is the correct behaviour: navigating on an
unmeasured mount transform produces confidently wrong positions.

============================================================================
ORDER OF OPERATIONS - THE MIRROR CHECK IS NOT OPTIONAL AND NOT LAST
============================================================================
  1. --mirror-check   turn ~90 deg by hand. Settles LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN.
  2. --level          static scan. Is the scan plane striking the floor?
  3. --yaw            several straight pushes. THE number.
  4. --lever          turn in place. x/y, weakly. A cross-check on a ruler.
  5. --apply          write config.env, once, after confirming.
  6. --verify         push again; the residual must now read ~0.

Everything except --mirror-check, --level and --report REFUSES TO RUN until
the mirror check has passed, because a mirror is NOT a rigid transform - no
value of laser_yaw can undo one - and measuring yaw against a mirrored world
returns a confident, plausible, wrong number. That refusal is the whole reason
the ordering is enforced in code rather than written in a runbook.

============================================================================
THE ONE THING THIS PROGRAM CANNOT SEE
============================================================================
Applying FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW changes the TF TREE. It does not change one byte of
/scan_lidar - the scan arrives in the laser frame either way. So --verify does
NOT expect the raw measurement to move to zero. It re-measures the mount yaw
from fresh pushes, subtracts what has actually been applied, and asserts the
RESIDUAL is ~0:

    residual = measured_mount_yaw - applied_laser_yaw   ->  must be ~0

A sign error does not make that residual merely wrong, it makes it almost
exactly TWICE the applied value, which is why --verify can name the failure
instead of just reporting a mismatch. Sign errors are this project's signature
failure: the odometry pose sign, the turn wire sign, the duty polarity, the
encoder polarity and LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN itself. Each one was silent, each one
produced perfectly plausible numbers, and each one cost days.

--verify also reads the LIVE transform off /tf_static, so "you edited
config.env and never restarted fpms-tf" is caught as well.

============================================================================
THE CONVENTION PROBES - READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANY SIGN IN THIS FILE
============================================================================
fpms_scanmatch's docstrings define the METHOD. They do not, on their own,
pin down which way its numbers point, and two of them do not point the way a
first reading suggests. Measured against synthetic scans, on this build:

  estimate_rotation(A, B)  returns the CHASSIS rotation, i.e. MINUS the
      apparent rotation of the world. Feed a profile shifted so every feature
      moves -10 degrees and it returns +10. But mirror_verdict's docstring is
      explicit that it wants the APPARENT rotation ("every static feature
      appears to rotate by -dtheta ... on a correctly-handed scan the two have
      OPPOSITE signs"). Wiring one straight into the other inverts the verdict:
      a correct scanner reports MIRRORED and a mirrored one reports OK.

  estimate_translation(A, B, rot)  also wants the APPARENT rotation. Given the
      raw return above, ICP diverges: measured 0.43 m RMS against 0.014 m with
      the sign the other way, on the same synthetic pair.

  lever_arm_from_rotation(...)  then returns MINUS the offset.

  yaw_from_straight_push(...)  is correct as documented and needs no
      correction. That is the number that actually gets written, and it was
      checked first.

So this file does NOT call estimate_rotation directly anywhere, and it does
NOT hardcode a compensating minus sign - a hardcoded sign would silently
invert the day somebody fixes the estimator. Instead:

  * rotation_convention() shifts a synthetic profile by a KNOWN number of bins
    and reads back which sign comes out. That probe needs no geometry, no
    hardware and no assumptions - a feature moved from bin j+k to bin j has
    moved by -k bins and there is nothing to argue about.
  * fit_motion() then runs ICP with BOTH signs and keeps the one that actually
    fits, so the alignment is decided by residual and not by belief.
  * lever_arm() picks between +L and -L by predicting the translation each
    would produce and comparing against the one observed.

Every one of those is an outcome check. If the estimator is corrected later,
all three follow it automatically. If it is changed into something neither
convention explains, rotation_convention() refuses to let the tool run at all,
which is the correct response to an estimator nobody understands any more.

What it still cannot do is confirm the CONVENTION. If the whole stack agreed
on a mirrored idea of which way is positive, every number here would be
self-consistent and wrong together. The end of --verify prints the one physical
check that closes that loop, and it needs eyes, not software.

============================================================================
DEPENDENCIES AND ENVIRONMENT
============================================================================
Python 3.10, stdlib + numpy + rclpy. The estimator lives in
/usr/local/lib/fpms/fpms_scanmatch.py and is CALLED, never reimplemented - it
is pure numpy specifically so it can be tested against synthetic scans on any
machine, and duplicating its geometry here would put a second, untested copy of
the sign conventions on the rover.

    source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
    ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20 RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_fastrtps_cpp \
    FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/etc/fpms/fastdds_udp_only.xml \
    fpms-calibrate-lidar --mirror-check

Those three variables are defaulted here if unset. The DDS profile is not
decoration: without it Fast DDS prefers shared memory, whose segments do not
survive across process eras, and the result is discovery that succeeds while
NO DATA FLOWS - a subscriber that sees a publisher and receives nothing, with
no error anywhere.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import math
import os
import re
import statistics
import sys
import threading
import time

# ===========================================================================
# PATHS
# ===========================================================================
# Every one of these is overridable by environment so the refusal paths can be
# exercised off-robot against a stub estimator and a scratch config, which is
# the only way any of this got tested before the board was available.
LIB_DIR = os.environ.get("FPMS_LIB_DIR", "/usr/local/lib/fpms")
CONFIG = os.environ.get("FPMS_CONFIG", "/etc/fpms/config.env")
STATE_FILE = os.environ.get("FPMS_LIDAR_CALIB_FILE",
                            "/var/lib/fpms/lidar-calib.json")
DDS_PROFILE = os.environ.get("FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE",
                             "/etc/fpms/fastdds_udp_only.xml")

SCAN_TOPIC = os.environ.get("FPMS_SCAN_TOPIC", "/scan_lidar")
IMU_TOPIC = os.environ.get("FPMS_IMU_TOPIC", "/imu")
TF_STATIC_TOPIC = "/tf_static"

BASE_LINK = "base_link"
LASER_FRAME = "laser_frame"

# fpms-lidar-ros.service runs `python3 /home/ubuntu/fpms_lidar_ros.py`. That
# file is owned by another agent: it is READ here (for LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN and
# LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG) and never written.
LIDAR_ROS_CANDIDATES = tuple(p for p in (
    os.environ.get("FPMS_LIDAR_ROS_PY"),
    "/home/ubuntu/fpms_lidar_ros.py",
    "/usr/local/lib/fpms/fpms_lidar_ros.py",
    "/opt/fpms/fpms_lidar_ros.py",
) if p)

# The six keys fpms-tf.service passes to the launch file, in launch order.
LASER_KEYS = ("FPMS_TF_LASER_X", "FPMS_TF_LASER_Y", "FPMS_TF_LASER_Z",
              "FPMS_TF_LASER_ROLL", "FPMS_TF_LASER_PITCH", "FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW")
MEASURED_KEY = "FPMS_TF_OFFSETS_MEASURED"

# ===========================================================================
# THRESHOLDS - every one of them has a reason, and the reason is written down
# ===========================================================================

# --- freshness -------------------------------------------------------------
# The agent publishes ON A TIMER, not on data, so ARRIVAL DOES NOT IMPLY
# FRESHNESS. A dead scanner emits all-zero ranges, zero converts to inf ("no
# return"), and the result reads as a completely clear 360 degrees. That is the
# most dangerous possible rendering of "the sensor is broken", so this program
# checks the scan is actually CHANGING rather than merely arriving.
SCAN_MAX_AGE_S = 2.0          # older than this and the feed has stopped
SCAN_IDENTICAL_LIMIT = 4      # consecutive bit-identical scans = frozen
SCAN_MIN_FINITE_FRAC = 0.05   # below this the scan carries no geometry at all
SCAN_MIN_BINS = 32

# --- mirror check ----------------------------------------------------------
MIRROR_TARGET_DEG = 90.0      # comfortably above the estimator's 15 deg floor
MIRROR_WINDOW_S = 45.0

# --- straight push ---------------------------------------------------------
PUSH_TARGET_M = 0.50
PUSH_WINDOW_S = 45.0
PUSH_MIN_M = 0.25             # yaw_from_straight_push saturates at ~0.20 m
PUSH_WARN_M = 0.35
# A push that also TURNS the chassis mixes rotation into the apparent motion
# direction, which is the whole measurement. De-rotation removes most of it;
# these bound what is left.
PUSH_TURN_WARN_DEG = 2.0
PUSH_TURN_REJECT_DEG = 5.0
# ICP quality. A push across a metre of a cluttered room loses correspondences
# on both ends; these say when what is left stopped being a fit.
PUSH_MAX_RMS_M = 0.06
PUSH_MIN_INLIERS = 30
PUSH_MIN_CONFIDENCE = 0.6     # from yaw_from_straight_push

MIN_PUSHES = 3                # a median of two samples is not a median
# Peak-to-peak spread across accepted pushes. The launch file wants mount yaw
# known to better than ~0.5 deg; 1.5 deg peak-to-peak over >=3 pushes puts the
# median comfortably inside that. Wider than this and the samples disagree
# about something real - a loose mount, a rover that was steered, or a room
# with too little structure - and no median rescues that.
YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG = 1.5

# --- settle detection ------------------------------------------------------
# A healthy gyro on this hardware reads EXACTLY 0.0 when parked, because of a
# +/-0.01 rad/s deadband. That is not a fault and must never be reported as
# one; it is, however, an excellent "the operator has stopped moving it"
# signal.
SETTLE_RATE_RAD_S = 0.03
SETTLE_HOLD_S = 1.2
LIVE_HZ = 2.0                 # live guidance updates per second

# --- verify ----------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFY_RESIDUAL_PASS_DEG = 1.0
VERIFY_TF_MATCH_DEG = 0.2     # config.env vs the live /tf_static transform

MIRROR_STALE_WARN_DAYS = 7.0
YAW_STALE_WARN_DAYS = 7.0

RAD2DEG = 180.0 / math.pi
DEG2RAD = math.pi / 180.0

# ===========================================================================
# THE ESTIMATOR
# ===========================================================================
# Called, not reimplemented. FPMS_LIB_DIR is prepended (not appended) so a stub
# directory wins over the installed module during offline testing.
if LIB_DIR not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, LIB_DIR)

try:
    import fpms_scanmatch as sm
except ImportError as _exc:  # pragma: no cover - environment failure
    sys.stderr.write(
        "fpms-calibrate-lidar: cannot import fpms_scanmatch from %s (%s).\n"
        "  It ships at /usr/local/lib/fpms/fpms_scanmatch.py and needs numpy.\n"
        "  Check:  python3 -c 'import numpy; print(numpy.__version__)'\n"
        % (LIB_DIR, _exc))
    raise SystemExit(1)


# ===========================================================================
# OUTPUT
# ===========================================================================

def log(*a):
    print(*a, flush=True)


def live(msg):
    """One rewritable line. The operator is holding a robot with both hands.

    Everything time-critical goes through here rather than into a scrolling
    wall of text they cannot read while pushing.
    """
    sys.stdout.write("\r    " + msg.ljust(74))
    sys.stdout.flush()


def live_done(msg=""):
    sys.stdout.write("\r" + " " * 80 + "\r")
    if msg:
        sys.stdout.write("    " + msg + "\n")
    sys.stdout.flush()


def hr(title=""):
    log("\n" + "=" * 76)
    if title:
        log(title)
        log("=" * 76)


def ask(prompt):
    try:
        return input(prompt).strip()
    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
        log("\naborted; nothing written")
        sys.exit(1)


def press_enter(prompt):
    try:
        input(prompt)
        return True
    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
        log("\naborted; nothing written")
        sys.exit(1)


# ===========================================================================
# ANGLES
# ===========================================================================

def wrap_deg(d):
    return (d + 180.0) % 360.0 - 180.0


def circular_median_deg(samples_deg):
    """Median of angles, done so a mount at +/-180 does not average to zero.

    A scanner bolted on backwards produces samples like +179.6 and -179.8. Their
    arithmetic median is -0.1 degrees, which is not a small error - it is the
    exact opposite answer, and it looks entirely reasonable. Everything is
    unwrapped relative to the first sample first.

    Returns (median_deg, spread_peak_to_peak_deg, unwrapped_samples).
    """
    if not samples_deg:
        return None, None, []
    ref = samples_deg[0]
    un = [ref + wrap_deg(s - ref) for s in samples_deg]
    med = statistics.median(un)
    spread = max(un) - min(un)
    return wrap_deg(med), spread, un


def yaw_from_quat(x, y, z, w):
    return math.atan2(2.0 * (w * z + x * y), 1.0 - 2.0 * (y * y + z * z))


def fmt_rad(r):
    return "%+.5f rad (%+.2f deg)" % (r, r * RAD2DEG)


# ===========================================================================
# CONVENTION PROBES - measure which way the estimator points, never assume it
# ===========================================================================
# See "THE CONVENTION PROBES" in the module docstring for why this exists. In
# one line: wiring estimate_rotation straight into mirror_verdict inverts the
# verdict, and a tool that reports MIRRORED for a correct scanner is worse than
# no tool at all.

_ROT_CONVENTION = None
_ROT_CONVENTION_DETAIL = ""

# The probe shifts a synthetic profile by this many bins. Big enough to be
# unambiguous, small enough that no sane max_shift_bins default could clip it.
_PROBE_SHIFT_BINS = 10
_PROBE_BINS = 360


def rotation_convention():
    """+1 if estimate_rotation returns the APPARENT rotation, -1 if it negates.

    No geometry, no hardware, no room: build a profile, roll it by a known
    number of bins, and read back the sign. A feature that was in bin j+k and
    is now in bin j has moved by -k bins. There is nothing in that statement to
    get wrong, which is exactly why the probe is built this way rather than out
    of a simulated room whose own conventions could be as wrong as the thing it
    is checking.

    Raises SystemExit if the estimator answers something neither convention
    explains. That is not paranoia: every measurement this program makes flows
    through this sign, and the failure mode is a confident inverted answer.
    """
    global _ROT_CONVENTION, _ROT_CONVENTION_DETAIL
    if _ROT_CONVENTION is not None:
        return _ROT_CONVENTION

    n = _PROBE_BINS
    inc = 2.0 * math.pi / n
    a = [2.0 + 0.9 * math.sin(3.0 * j * inc)
         + 0.5 * math.cos(7.0 * j * inc + 1.1)
         + 0.2 * math.sin(11.0 * j * inc) for j in range(n)]
    k = _PROBE_SHIFT_BINS
    # b[j] = a[j+k]: whatever sat at bearing bin j+k now sits at bin j, so
    # every feature moved by -k bins. The apparent rotation is -k*inc.
    b = [a[(j + k) % n] for j in range(n)]
    want = -k * inc

    try:
        rot, conf = sm.estimate_rotation(a, b, inc)
    except Exception as exc:
        raise SystemExit(
            "refuse: fpms_scanmatch.estimate_rotation raised %r on a synthetic "
            "probe. Nothing measured against it could be trusted." % (exc,))

    tol = 0.25 * abs(want)
    if abs(rot - want) <= tol:
        _ROT_CONVENTION = 1
        _ROT_CONVENTION_DETAIL = (
            "estimate_rotation returns the APPARENT rotation directly "
            "(probe: %+.1f deg expected, %+.1f deg returned, confidence %.2f)"
            % (want * RAD2DEG, rot * RAD2DEG, conf))
    elif abs(rot + want) <= tol:
        _ROT_CONVENTION = -1
        _ROT_CONVENTION_DETAIL = (
            "estimate_rotation returns the NEGATED apparent rotation, i.e. the "
            "chassis rotation (probe: %+.1f deg expected, %+.1f deg returned, "
            "confidence %.2f). Every use below is negated to match "
            "mirror_verdict's documented convention."
            % (want * RAD2DEG, rot * RAD2DEG, conf))
    else:
        raise SystemExit(
            "refuse: the rotation probe shifted a synthetic scan by %+.1f deg "
            "and fpms_scanmatch.estimate_rotation returned %+.1f deg "
            "(confidence %.2f). That is neither convention, so this program "
            "cannot know which way its own measurements point. Nothing will be "
            "measured until that is explained."
            % (want * RAD2DEG, rot * RAD2DEG, conf))
    return _ROT_CONVENTION


def convention_detail():
    rotation_convention()
    return _ROT_CONVENTION_DETAIL


def apparent_rotation(ranges_a, ranges_b, angle_increment):
    """(radians, confidence) of how far the WORLD appears to have turned.

    Positive means static features swept counter-clockwise in the scan, which a
    chassis turning clockwise produces. This is the quantity mirror_verdict
    documents that it wants and the quantity estimate_translation needs.
    """
    rot, conf = sm.estimate_rotation(ranges_a, ranges_b, angle_increment)
    return rotation_convention() * rot, conf


def fit_motion(scan_a, scan_b, iterations=25, chassis_rot_rad=None):
    """Rotation + translation between two scans, with the alignment sign decided
    by which one actually fits.

    The probe already says which sign to use. This runs ICP both ways anyway
    and keeps the better residual, because a residual is an outcome and a
    convention is a belief. When the two disagree on a rotation big enough to
    tell them apart, that is reported rather than quietly resolved.
    """
    if chassis_rot_rad is not None:
        # DE-ROTATE WITH THE GYRO, not with the scan correlation.
        #
        # estimate_rotation() is confounded by translation: a straight push
        # sweeps near features past faster than far ones, and the correlation
        # cannot tell that from a turn. Measured on synthetic scans with a
        # known answer, over 36 pushes scored exactly as this function scores
        # them:
        #
        #     de-rotation source   accepted   worst error
        #     gyro                   30/36        0.56 deg
        #     scan correlation        6/36        4.28 deg
        #
        # Worse than the average: one push passed EVERY published gate and was
        # still 4.28 deg (45 mm) out, because the correlation reported 4.19 deg
        # of turn on a push with no turn in it - inside PUSH_TURN_REJECT_DEG,
        # with an rms inside PUSH_MAX_RMS_M and confidence 0.99. The gates are
        # tuned to catch a STEERED push and cannot distinguish one from a
        # translation-confounded estimate.
        #
        # The gyro does not have this failure mode: it never sees the scan. It
        # is the same independent witness the handedness check leans on, and
        # PUSH_TARGET_M = 0.50 is precisely where the confounding is worst.
        rot = -float(chassis_rot_rad)   # chassis -> apparent world rotation
        conf = 1.0
    else:
        rot, conf = apparent_rotation(scan_a.ranges, scan_b.ranges,
                                      scan_b.angle_increment)
    A, B = scan_a.xy(), scan_b.xy()
    tried = []
    for cand in (rot, -rot):
        dx, dy, rms, n = sm.estimate_translation(A, B, cand,
                                                 iterations=iterations)
        tried.append({"rot": cand, "dx": dx, "dy": dy, "rms": rms,
                      "inliers": n})
    best = min(tried, key=lambda r: r["rms"])
    other = tried[1] if best is tried[0] else tried[0]

    note = ""
    if best["rot"] != rot and abs(rot) * RAD2DEG > 2.0 and \
            best["rms"] < 0.5 * other["rms"]:
        note = ("the ICP fits far better with the OPPOSITE rotation sign to "
                "the one the convention probe predicted (%.4f m vs %.4f m "
                "residual). Something about the estimator has changed; treat "
                "every number in this run as suspect."
                % (best["rms"], other["rms"]))
    return {"rot": best["rot"], "rot_confidence": conf,
            "dx": best["dx"], "dy": best["dy"], "rms": best["rms"],
            "inliers": best["inliers"], "alt_rms": other["rms"], "note": note}


def lever_arm(dx, dy, rot_apparent):
    """(lx, ly, err, detail) - the scanner offset, with its sign resolved.

    fpms_scanmatch.lever_arm_from_rotation returns an answer whose sign depends
    on which rotation convention it was handed, so the candidate and its
    negation are both run FORWARD through the geometry and the one that
    predicts the translation actually observed is kept:

        a scanner at offset L, on a chassis rotated so the world appears to
        turn by `rot`, sees the world translate by (R(rot) - I) * L

    That forward relation is two lines of trigonometry and is checked against
    the observed (dx, dy) rather than assumed.
    """
    lx, ly, err = sm.lever_arm_from_rotation(dx, dy, rot_apparent)
    if err or lx is None:
        return None, None, err or "no solution", ""
    c, s = math.cos(rot_apparent), math.sin(rot_apparent)
    best = None
    for cand in ((lx, ly), (-lx, -ly)):
        px = (c - 1.0) * cand[0] - s * cand[1]
        py = s * cand[0] + (c - 1.0) * cand[1]
        res = math.hypot(px - dx, py - dy)
        if best is None or res < best[1]:
            best = (cand, res)
    (blx, bly), res = best
    flipped = (blx, bly) != (lx, ly)
    detail = ("forward check: the offset that predicts the observed "
              "(%+.3f, %+.3f) m of apparent translation is (%+.3f, %+.3f), "
              "residual %.4f m%s"
              % (dx, dy, blx, bly, res,
                 " (the estimator's raw sign was the other one)"
                 if flipped else ""))
    return blx, bly, None, detail


# ===========================================================================
# config.env
# ===========================================================================

def read_config(path=None):
    """Read config.env the way the services read it: file only, no env overlay.

    Deliberately NOT overlaid with os.environ. fpms-tf.service takes these
    values from the FILE via EnvironmentFile=, so what this program reports and
    writes has to be what the file says, not what the current shell happens to
    export. (fpms_cored.py and fpms_charact.py overlay env on top; the mission
    executor and this program do not. That disagreement is documented at the
    top of config.env and it has bitten people.)
    """
    path = path or CONFIG
    cfg = {}
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                    continue
                k, v = line.split("=", 1)
                cfg[k.strip()] = v.strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    except OSError:
        pass
    return cfg


def config_float(cfg, key, default=None):
    raw = cfg.get(key)
    if raw is None or raw == "":
        return default
    try:
        return float(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return default


def write_config_values(updates, path=None):
    """Set keys in config.env in place, preserving order, comments and mode.

    Refuses rather than repairs on a CR: EnvironmentFile= keeps the \\r, so
    FPMS_TF_OFFSETS_MEASURED=1\\r is not '1', fpms-tf refuses to start, and the
    comparison that rejected it prints two strings that look identical. Adding
    LF-only lines to a CRLF file would produce exactly that, half the time.

    Returns (ok, message).
    """
    path = path or CONFIG
    try:
        with open(path, "r", newline="") as fh:
            raw = fh.read()
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, "cannot read %s (%s)" % (path, exc)

    if "\r" in raw:
        return False, (
            "%s contains carriage returns. Writing to it would leave mixed "
            "line endings, and a value ending in an invisible \\r is the "
            "failure where two identical-looking strings do not compare "
            "equal. Fix it first:  sudo sed -i 's/\\r$//' %s" % (path, path))

    try:
        mode = os.stat(path).st_mode & 0o7777
    except OSError:
        mode = 0o640

    lines = raw.split("\n")
    remaining = dict(updates)
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not stripped or stripped.startswith("#") or "=" not in stripped:
            continue
        key = stripped.split("=", 1)[0].strip()
        if key in remaining:
            lines[i] = "%s=%s" % (key, remaining.pop(key))

    if remaining:
        tail = []
        if lines and lines[-1] == "":
            lines.pop()
        tail.append("")
        tail.append("# Added by fpms-calibrate-lidar on %s because the key was "
                    "absent." % time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"))
        for k in updates:
            if k in remaining:
                tail.append("%s=%s" % (k, remaining[k]))
        tail.append("")
        lines.extend(tail)

    body = "\n".join(lines)

    # Back the file up first, at the SAME mode. It holds the broker password
    # after first boot, so a 0644 backup would publish that to every account on
    # the box.
    backup = "%s.bak-%s" % (path, time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S"))
    tmp = path + ".fpms-tmp"
    try:
        fd = os.open(backup, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, mode)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", newline="\n") as fh:
            fh.write(raw)
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, mode)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", newline="\n") as fh:
            fh.write(body)
            fh.flush()
            os.fsync(fh.fileno())
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except PermissionError:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return False, (
            "no permission to write %s (it is root:root 0640).\n"
            "  Re-run the same command under sudo:  sudo -E %s --apply\n"
            "  -E matters: it keeps the sourced ROS environment."
            % (path, sys.argv[0]))
    except OSError as exc:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return False, "write failed (%s); %s is unchanged" % (exc, path)

    # Verify the OUTCOME, not the exit status of the write. Re-read the file and
    # confirm every key now reads back exactly as intended.
    after = read_config(path)
    wrong = [k for k, v in updates.items() if after.get(k) != v]
    if wrong:
        return False, ("wrote %s but %s did not read back as intended - the "
                       "backup is at %s" % (path, ", ".join(wrong), backup))
    return True, "wrote %s (backup: %s)" % (path, backup)


# ===========================================================================
# state file - what has been measured, and when, and from what evidence
# ===========================================================================

def read_state():
    try:
        with open(STATE_FILE) as fh:
            d = json.load(fh)
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def save_state(state):
    state["updated_at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")
    target = STATE_FILE
    try:
        d = os.path.dirname(target)
        if d and not os.path.isdir(d):
            os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = target + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w", newline="\n") as fh:
            json.dump(state, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            fh.write("\n")
        os.replace(tmp, target)
        # Running one subcommand under sudo and the next as ubuntu must not
        # leave a file the unprivileged run cannot rewrite.
        try:
            if hasattr(os, "geteuid") and os.geteuid() == 0:
                st = os.stat(os.path.dirname(target) or ".")
                os.chown(target, st.st_uid, st.st_gid)
            os.chmod(target, 0o664)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return target
    except OSError:
        alt = os.path.expanduser("~/.fpms_lidar_calib.json")
        try:
            with open(alt, "w", newline="\n") as fh:
                json.dump(state, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
                fh.write("\n")
            log("\n  (could not write %s; state kept in %s. Later runs will "
                "NOT see it unless you set FPMS_LIDAR_CALIB_FILE=%s)"
                % (target, alt, alt))
            return alt
        except OSError as exc:
            log("\n  WARNING: could not save state anywhere (%s). This run's "
                "result exists only on screen." % exc)
            return None


def stamp():
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")


def age_days(iso):
    if not iso:
        return None
    try:
        t = time.mktime(time.strptime(iso, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"))
    except (ValueError, OverflowError):
        return None
    return max(0.0, (time.time() - t) / 86400.0)


# ===========================================================================
# fpms_lidar_ros.py constants - READ ONLY. That file belongs to another agent.
# ===========================================================================

def read_lidar_ros_constants():
    """Parse LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN / LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG out of the source.

    Parsed rather than imported: importing pulls in whatever that module does at
    import time, and this program has no business executing another service's
    code to read two numbers off it.

    Returns (path, rotation_sign, zero_offset_deg); any element may be None.
    """
    for path in LIDAR_ROS_CANDIDATES:
        try:
            with open(path) as fh:
                text = fh.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
        sign = zero = None
        m = re.search(r"^\s*LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN\s*=\s*([-+]?\d+)",
                      text, re.M)
        if m:
            sign = int(m.group(1))
        m = re.search(r"^\s*LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG\s*=\s*([-+]?[\d.]+)",
                      text, re.M)
        if m:
            try:
                zero = float(m.group(1))
            except ValueError:
                zero = None
        return path, sign, zero
    return None, None, None


# ===========================================================================
# ROS ENVIRONMENT
# ===========================================================================

def prepare_ros_env():
    """Default the three variables this rover cannot work without, and refuse
    on a domain mismatch rather than starting a node nobody can hear.

    A node on the wrong ROS_DOMAIN_ID starts cleanly, subscribes successfully,
    and talks to nobody. Same guard as fpms_tf.launch.py, fpms_odom_tf.py and
    deadband_sweep.py, so all of them fail the same way.
    """
    notes = []
    cur = os.environ.get("ROS_DOMAIN_ID")
    if cur is None:
        os.environ["ROS_DOMAIN_ID"] = "20"
        notes.append("ROS_DOMAIN_ID was unset; using 20 (mandatory here)")
    elif cur.strip() != "20":
        raise SystemExit(
            "refuse: ROS_DOMAIN_ID=%r in this shell but this rover is "
            "hard-wired to 20. A node on the wrong domain starts cleanly, "
            "publishes and subscribes successfully, and talks to nobody."
            % (cur,))

    rmw = os.environ.get("RMW_IMPLEMENTATION")
    if not rmw:
        os.environ["RMW_IMPLEMENTATION"] = "rmw_fastrtps_cpp"
        notes.append("RMW_IMPLEMENTATION defaulted to rmw_fastrtps_cpp")
    elif rmw != "rmw_fastrtps_cpp":
        notes.append("RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=%s - the rest of the stack runs "
                     "rmw_fastrtps_cpp; two RMWs do not interoperate" % rmw)

    prof = os.environ.get("FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE")
    if not prof:
        os.environ["FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE"] = DDS_PROFILE
        prof = DDS_PROFILE
        notes.append("FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE defaulted to %s"
                     % DDS_PROFILE)
    if not os.path.exists(prof):
        notes.append(
            "WARNING: %s does not exist. Without it Fast DDS prefers shared "
            "memory, whose segments do not survive across process eras: "
            "discovery succeeds and NO DATA FLOWS, with no error anywhere. If "
            "this program sees a publisher and receives nothing, that is why."
            % prof)
    return notes


# ===========================================================================
# ROS INPUT - subscriptions only
# ===========================================================================

class Scan(object):
    """One captured scan, decoupled from the callback."""

    __slots__ = ("t", "ranges", "angle_min", "angle_increment", "seq",
                 "finite_frac")

    def __init__(self, t, ranges, angle_min, angle_increment, seq):
        self.t = t
        self.ranges = ranges
        self.angle_min = angle_min
        self.angle_increment = angle_increment
        self.seq = seq
        n = len(ranges)
        good = 0
        for r in ranges:
            if r == r and r not in (float("inf"), float("-inf")) and r > 0.0:
                good += 1
        self.finite_frac = (float(good) / n) if n else 0.0

    def xy(self):
        return sm.scan_to_xy(self.ranges, self.angle_min, self.angle_increment)


class Listener(object):
    """Subscribe-only view of the scanner and the gyro. Creates NO publisher.

    Topics:
      /scan_lidar   sensor_msgs/LaserScan  (fpms_lidar_ros.py)
      /imu          sensor_msgs/Imu        (micro-ROS agent)
      /tf_static    tf2_msgs/TFMessage     (read by --verify only)

    QoS is BEST_EFFORT on the two sensor topics. That is the maximally
    compatible choice: a best-effort SUBSCRIBER matches both a reliable and a
    best-effort publisher, whereas a reliable subscriber is silently
    incompatible with a best-effort publisher and shows up as "the topic exists
    and I receive nothing" - which on this rover is already the signature of
    two other faults and does not need a third source.
    """

    def __init__(self, want_tf=False):
        import rclpy
        from rclpy.qos import (QoSProfile, ReliabilityPolicy, DurabilityPolicy,
                               HistoryPolicy)
        from sensor_msgs.msg import Imu, LaserScan

        self._rclpy = rclpy
        rclpy.init(args=None)
        self.node = rclpy.create_node("fpms_calibrate_lidar")

        sensor_qos = QoSProfile(reliability=ReliabilityPolicy.BEST_EFFORT,
                                durability=DurabilityPolicy.VOLATILE,
                                history=HistoryPolicy.KEEP_LAST, depth=10)

        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.scan = None
        self.scan_n = 0
        self.scan_changed_n = 0
        self.identical_run = 0
        self._last_ranges = None

        self.gyro_int = 0.0
        self.gyro_z = 0.0
        self.imu_n = 0
        self._gyro_t = None

        self.tf_static = {}          # (parent, child) -> transform dict
        self.tf_n = 0

        self.node.create_subscription(LaserScan, SCAN_TOPIC, self._on_scan,
                                      sensor_qos)
        self.node.create_subscription(Imu, IMU_TOPIC, self._on_imu, sensor_qos)

        self.tf_available = False
        if want_tf:
            try:
                from tf2_msgs.msg import TFMessage
                # static transforms are latched: TRANSIENT_LOCAL or a
                # subscriber that starts late sees nothing at all.
                tf_qos = QoSProfile(
                    reliability=ReliabilityPolicy.RELIABLE,
                    durability=DurabilityPolicy.TRANSIENT_LOCAL,
                    history=HistoryPolicy.KEEP_LAST, depth=100)
                self.node.create_subscription(TFMessage, TF_STATIC_TOPIC,
                                              self._on_tf, tf_qos)
                self.tf_available = True
            except Exception as exc:
                log("  (tf2_msgs unavailable: %s - the live TF cross-check "
                    "will be skipped)" % exc)

        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._spin = threading.Thread(target=self._spin_loop, daemon=True)
        self._spin.start()

        self.publisher_selfcheck = self._assert_no_publishers()

    # -- the promise at the top of this file, verified ----------------------

    def _assert_no_publishers(self):
        """Ask the node what it publishes and exit if the answer is not 'nothing'.

        rclpy gives every node /rosout and /parameter_events for free; anything
        else on this list would mean an actuation surface exists in a program
        whose entire safety argument is that one cannot.
        """
        allowed = {"/rosout", "/parameter_events"}
        try:
            pubs = self.node.get_publisher_names_and_types_by_node(
                self.node.get_name(), self.node.get_namespace())
        except Exception as exc:
            return "not verifiable on this rclpy (%s)" % exc
        extra = sorted(t for t, _ in pubs if t not in allowed)
        if extra:
            self.close()
            raise SystemExit(
                "refuse: this node is publishing %s. fpms-calibrate-lidar must "
                "publish to NOTHING - that is its entire safety argument. "
                "Something added a publisher; find it before running again."
                % (", ".join(extra),))
        return "verified: publishes nothing but %s" % ", ".join(sorted(allowed))

    # -- callbacks ----------------------------------------------------------

    def _spin_loop(self):
        while not self._stop.is_set():
            try:
                self._rclpy.spin_once(self.node, timeout_sec=0.1)
            except Exception:
                time.sleep(0.05)

    def _on_scan(self, msg):
        ranges = tuple(float(r) for r in msg.ranges)
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self.lock:
            if self._last_ranges is not None and ranges == self._last_ranges:
                self.identical_run += 1
            else:
                if self._last_ranges is not None:
                    self.scan_changed_n += 1
                self.identical_run = 0
            self._last_ranges = ranges
            self.scan_n += 1
            self.scan = Scan(now, ranges, float(msg.angle_min),
                             float(msg.angle_increment), self.scan_n)

    def _on_imu(self, msg):
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self.lock:
            self.gyro_z = float(msg.angular_velocity.z)
            if self._gyro_t is not None:
                dt = now - self._gyro_t
                # Bound the step: a stall in the spin thread must not integrate
                # one sample over several seconds.
                if 0.0 < dt < 0.5:
                    self.gyro_int += self.gyro_z * dt
            self._gyro_t = now
            self.imu_n += 1

    def _on_tf(self, msg):
        with self.lock:
            for tr in msg.transforms:
                key = (tr.header.frame_id.lstrip("/"),
                       tr.child_frame_id.lstrip("/"))
                q = tr.transform.rotation
                t = tr.transform.translation
                self.tf_static[key] = {
                    "x": float(t.x), "y": float(t.y), "z": float(t.z),
                    "yaw": yaw_from_quat(float(q.x), float(q.y), float(q.z),
                                         float(q.w)),
                    "qx": float(q.x), "qy": float(q.y), "qz": float(q.z),
                    "qw": float(q.w),
                }
                self.tf_n += 1

    # -- accessors ----------------------------------------------------------

    def capture(self):
        with self.lock:
            return self.scan

    def gyro(self):
        with self.lock:
            return self.gyro_int, self.gyro_z, self.imu_n

    def reset_gyro(self):
        with self.lock:
            self.gyro_int = 0.0
            self._gyro_t = None

    def tf_lookup(self, parent, child):
        with self.lock:
            return dict(self.tf_static.get((parent, child), {})) or None

    # -- health -------------------------------------------------------------

    def scan_health(self):
        """(ok, detail). The refusal that keeps a dead scanner from being read
        as a clear 360 degrees."""
        with self.lock:
            s = self.scan
            n = self.scan_n
            ident = self.identical_run
            changed = self.scan_changed_n
        if s is None:
            return False, ("no LaserScan has arrived on %s. Check: systemctl "
                           "status fpms-lidar-ros, and that the MQTT lidar "
                           "feed is alive (fpms-doctor no-lidar)" % SCAN_TOPIC)
        age = time.monotonic() - s.t
        if age > SCAN_MAX_AGE_S:
            return False, ("the last scan is %.1f s old; the feed has stopped. "
                           "fpms_lidar_ros.py deliberately stops publishing "
                           "rather than repeating a frozen scan." % age)
        if len(s.ranges) < SCAN_MIN_BINS:
            return False, "only %d bins in the scan; geometry needs at least %d" \
                          % (len(s.ranges), SCAN_MIN_BINS)
        if s.finite_frac < SCAN_MIN_FINITE_FRAC:
            return False, (
                "%.0f%% of ranges are finite. An all-inf scan is what a DEAD "
                "SCANNER looks like after zero-to-inf conversion, and it reads "
                "as a completely clear 360 degrees. Refusing to measure "
                "against it." % (100.0 * s.finite_frac))
        if ident >= SCAN_IDENTICAL_LIMIT:
            return False, (
                "the last %d scans are bit-for-bit identical. A real spinning "
                "scanner never repeats exactly; the agent publishes ON A TIMER "
                "so arrival does not imply freshness. This feed is frozen."
                % (ident + 1))
        if n >= 3 and changed == 0:
            return False, ("%d scans arrived and not one differed from the "
                           "previous - frozen feed" % n)
        return True, ("scan live: %d received, %d changed, %.0f%% finite "
                      "returns, age %.2f s"
                      % (n, changed, 100.0 * s.finite_frac, age))

    def wait_for_data(self, seconds=8.0, need_imu=True):
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        last = ""
        while time.monotonic() - t0 < seconds:
            with self.lock:
                sn, imn = self.scan_n, self.imu_n
            msg = "waiting for data: %d scans, %d imu" % (sn, imn)
            if msg != last:
                live(msg)
                last = msg
            if sn > 3 and (imn > 3 or not need_imu):
                live_done()
                return True
            time.sleep(0.2)
        live_done()
        return False

    def close(self):
        self._stop.set()
        try:
            self.node.destroy_node()
        except Exception:
            pass
        try:
            self._rclpy.shutdown()
        except Exception:
            pass


# ===========================================================================
# PRE-FLIGHT
# ===========================================================================

def start_listener(want_tf=False):
    """Bring up the node, or explain in one screen why it could not."""
    # Before any hardware: does the estimator point the way this program thinks
    # it does? Refuses here rather than after the operator has pushed a robot
    # around a room for ten minutes.
    log("  probe: %s" % convention_detail())
    notes = prepare_ros_env()
    for n in notes:
        log("  env: %s" % n)
    try:
        lis = Listener(want_tf=want_tf)
    except ImportError as exc:
        log("\nCannot import rclpy (%s)." % exc)
        log("  source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash")
        log("  then re-run:  %s %s" % (sys.argv[0], " ".join(sys.argv[1:])))
        return None
    except Exception as exc:
        log("\nCannot start a ROS node (%s)." % exc)
        log("  source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash")
        log("  ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20 must match the rest of the rover.")
        return None
    log("  node: %s" % lis.publisher_selfcheck)
    if not lis.wait_for_data(need_imu=True):
        with lis.lock:
            sn, imn = lis.scan_n, lis.imu_n
        log("\n  NOT ENOUGH DATA after 8 s: %d scans on %s, %d messages on %s."
            % (sn, SCAN_TOPIC, imn, IMU_TOPIC))
        if sn == 0:
            log("    no scan   -> systemctl status fpms-lidar-ros; "
                "fpms-doctor no-lidar")
        if imn == 0:
            log("    no imu    -> systemctl status micro-ros-agent; the board "
                "link is fragile and a re-link costs 90-225 s")
        log("    both zero -> suspect DDS. Check "
            "FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=%s is set and readable; shared "
            "memory gives you discovery with no data." % DDS_PROFILE)
        lis.close()
        return None
    ok, detail = lis.scan_health()
    log("  %s" % detail)
    if not ok:
        log("\n  REFUSING: %s" % detail)
        lis.close()
        return None
    return lis


def mirror_gate(state, what):
    """(ok, message). The gate every measuring subcommand passes through.

    A mirrored scan is not a rigid transform. No value of laser_yaw undoes one,
    so a yaw measured against a mirrored world is a confident wrong answer -
    which is worse than no answer, because it will be believed.
    """
    m = state.get("mirror") or {}
    if m.get("verdict") != "ok":
        got = m.get("verdict") or "never run"
        return False, (
            "%s refuses to run: the mirror check has not passed (%s).\n"
            "  A mirror is not a rigid transform - no value of laser_yaw can "
            "undo one - so anything measured now would be confidently wrong.\n"
            "  Run this first:   fpms-calibrate-lidar --mirror-check"
            % (what, got))

    path, sign, _zero = read_lidar_ros_constants()
    was = m.get("rotation_sign")
    if sign is not None and was is not None and sign != was:
        return False, (
            "%s refuses to run: the mirror check passed with "
            "LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN=%+d, but %s now says %+d.\n"
            "  Flipping that constant mirrors the scan, which invalidates the "
            "check and every measurement taken under it.\n"
            "  Re-run:   fpms-calibrate-lidar --mirror-check"
            % (what, was, path, sign))

    d = age_days(m.get("at"))
    if d is not None and d > MIRROR_STALE_WARN_DAYS:
        return True, ("  note: the mirror check is %.0f days old. If the "
                      "scanner has been unbolted since, re-run it." % d)
    return True, ""


# ===========================================================================
# LIVE TRACKING - the operator has both hands on the robot
# ===========================================================================

def track_turn(lis, ref, target_rad, window_s):
    """Watch a hand turn, narrating progress. Returns a result dict.

    Ends when the rover has passed ~80% of the target AND has been still for
    SETTLE_HOLD_S, or when the window expires. It does not ask the operator to
    press a key at the end: they are holding a robot.
    """
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    still_since = None
    last_rot, last_conf = 0.0, 0.0
    aborted = False
    try:
        while True:
            now = time.monotonic()
            elapsed = now - t0
            if elapsed > window_s:
                break
            snap = lis.capture()
            gyro_int, gyro_z, _ = lis.gyro()
            if snap is not None and snap.seq != ref.seq:
                last_rot, last_conf = apparent_rotation(
                    ref.ranges, snap.ranges, snap.angle_increment)
            # The gyro figure is the chassis; the scan figure is the world
            # sweeping the other way past it. Opposite signs is what RIGHT
            # looks like, so both are shown rather than one tidied number.
            live("turned %+.0f deg (gyro), world swept %+.0f deg (scan) - keep "
                 "going to %.0f deg  [%.0fs left]"
                 % (gyro_int * RAD2DEG, last_rot * RAD2DEG,
                    target_rad * RAD2DEG, max(0.0, window_s - elapsed)))
            if abs(gyro_int) >= 0.8 * abs(target_rad):
                if abs(gyro_z) < SETTLE_RATE_RAD_S:
                    if still_since is None:
                        still_since = now
                    elif now - still_since >= SETTLE_HOLD_S:
                        break
                else:
                    still_since = None
            time.sleep(1.0 / LIVE_HZ)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        aborted = True
    live_done()

    end = lis.capture()
    gyro_int, _, _ = lis.gyro()
    rot, conf = 0.0, 0.0
    if end is not None and ref is not None:
        rot, conf = apparent_rotation(ref.ranges, end.ranges,
                                      end.angle_increment)
    return {"ref": ref, "end": end, "scan_rot": rot, "scan_conf": conf,
            "gyro": gyro_int, "aborted": aborted,
            "elapsed": time.monotonic() - t0}


def track_push(lis, ref, target_m, window_s):
    """Watch a hand push, narrating distance travelled and heading drift."""
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    still_since = None
    dist = 0.0
    aborted = False
    try:
        while True:
            now = time.monotonic()
            elapsed = now - t0
            if elapsed > window_s:
                break
            snap = lis.capture()
            gyro_int, gyro_z, _ = lis.gyro()
            if snap is not None and snap.seq != ref.seq:
                # Cheap ICP for the live readout only; the accepted measurement
                # re-runs it at full iteration count.
                fit = fit_motion(ref, snap, iterations=10)
                dist = math.hypot(fit["dx"], fit["dy"])
            live("pushed %.2f m, heading drift %+.1f deg - keep going to "
                 "%.2f m  [%.0fs left]"
                 % (dist, gyro_int * RAD2DEG, target_m,
                    max(0.0, window_s - elapsed)))
            if dist >= 0.85 * target_m:
                if abs(gyro_z) < SETTLE_RATE_RAD_S:
                    if still_since is None:
                        still_since = now
                    elif now - still_since >= SETTLE_HOLD_S:
                        break
                else:
                    still_since = None
            time.sleep(1.0 / LIVE_HZ)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        aborted = True
    live_done()
    return {"ref": ref, "end": lis.capture(), "gyro": lis.gyro()[0],
            "aborted": aborted, "live_dist": dist}


# ===========================================================================
# --mirror-check
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_mirror_check(args):
    hr("MIRROR CHECK - is the scan mirrored? Nothing else runs until this passes")
    log("""
The chassis turns +dtheta; every static feature must therefore APPEAR to turn
-dtheta. Same sign in both means the scan is mirrored, and a mirror is not a
rigid transform: no value of laser_yaw can undo one. That is why this is first.

  1. Put the rover on the floor in a room with structure - walls, furniture,
     doorways. A bare corridor cannot settle this and the tool will say so.
  2. Press ENTER, then TURN IT ON THE SPOT by hand, about %.0f degrees, and
     let go. Direction does not matter.
  3. Do not lift it, do not slide it across the room. A turn, in place.

The screen narrates the turn as you make it. It stops on its own once you pass
%.0f degrees and hold still.""" % (MIRROR_TARGET_DEG,
                                   0.8 * MIRROR_TARGET_DEG))

    path, sign, zero = read_lidar_ros_constants()
    if sign is None:
        log("\n  NOTE: could not read LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN from any of %s."
            % (", ".join(LIDAR_ROS_CANDIDATES),))
        log("        The verdict below still stands; it just cannot record "
            "which sign it was taken under.")
    else:
        log("\n  %s currently has LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN=%+d, "
            "LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG=%s" % (path, sign, zero))

    lis = start_listener()
    if lis is None:
        return 2
    try:
        press_enter("\n  Press ENTER, then turn the rover...")
        ref = lis.capture()
        lis.reset_gyro()
        # A brief settle so the reference scan is not the one taken mid-nudge.
        time.sleep(0.3)
        ref = lis.capture()
        if ref is None:
            log("  no scan to use as a reference; aborting")
            return 2

        res = track_turn(lis, ref, MIRROR_TARGET_DEG * DEG2RAD,
                         args.window or MIRROR_WINDOW_S)

        ok, detail = lis.scan_health()
        if not ok:
            log("\n  REFUSING: the scan went bad during the turn - %s" % detail)
            return 1
        if res["end"] is None or res["end"].seq == ref.seq:
            log("\n  REFUSING: not one new scan arrived during the whole turn. "
                "The feed is not live.")
            return 1

        scan_deg = res["scan_rot"] * RAD2DEG
        gyro_deg = res["gyro"] * RAD2DEG
        log("\n  world swept  : %+.1f deg   (apparent rotation of static "
            "features, correlation confidence %.2f)"
            % (scan_deg, res["scan_conf"]))
        log("  chassis      : %+.1f deg   (integrated gyro angular_velocity.z)"
            % gyro_deg)
        log("  those two must have OPPOSITE signs. They are %s here."
            % ("opposite" if scan_deg * gyro_deg < 0 else "THE SAME"))

        if abs(gyro_deg) < 1.0 and abs(scan_deg) > 15.0:
            log("\n  The scan says the rover turned %.0f deg and the gyro "
                "integrated almost nothing." % abs(scan_deg))
            log("  That IS the dead-gyro signature - but do not conclude it "
                "from a PARKED reading: a healthy gyro on this hardware reads")
            log("  exactly 0.0 at rest because of a +/-0.01 rad/s deadband. "
                "Here the rover demonstrably moved, so this one counts.")
            log("  Check:  ros2 topic echo %s --field angular_velocity"
                % IMU_TOPIC)
            return 1

        if res["scan_conf"] < 0.15:
            log("\n  The correlation peak is weak (%.2f). This room may be too "
                "featureless - a bare corridor looks the same at every angle."
                % res["scan_conf"])
            log("  Move to somewhere with walls and furniture and re-run.")
            return 1

        verdict, why = sm.mirror_verdict(res["scan_rot"], res["gyro"])
        log("\n  VERDICT: %s" % verdict.upper())
        log("  %s" % why)

        state = read_state()
        state["mirror"] = {
            "verdict": verdict, "detail": why,
            "scan_deg": round(scan_deg, 2), "gyro_deg": round(gyro_deg, 2),
            "scan_confidence": round(res["scan_conf"], 3),
            "rotation_sign": sign, "zero_offset_deg": zero,
            "lidar_ros_path": path, "at": stamp(),
        }
        if verdict != "ok":
            # A failed or inconclusive check must destroy any earlier pass, or
            # the gate below would still be open on stale evidence.
            for key in ("yaw", "verify"):
                if key in state:
                    state[key + "_invalidated_by_mirror"] = state.pop(key)
        save_state(state)

        if verdict == "mirrored":
            log("""
  WHAT TO DO
  ----------
  Flip LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN in %s from %s to %s, restart the bridge, and run
  this check again. Do NOT try to absorb a mirror into laser_yaw - it cannot
  be done, and the attempt produces a number that fits one direction of travel
  and is wrong in every other.

      sudo systemctl restart fpms-lidar-ros
      fpms-calibrate-lidar --mirror-check

  That file is owned by another agent: report the result, do not edit around
  it if you are not the one who owns it.""" % (
                path or "fpms_lidar_ros.py",
                ("%+d" % sign) if sign is not None else "its current value",
                ("%+d" % -sign) if sign is not None else "the other sign"))
            return 1
        if verdict == "inconclusive":
            log("\n  Nothing has been settled. Re-run with a bigger, cleaner "
                "turn on the spot.")
            return 1

        log("\n  The scan is correctly handed. --yaw, --lever and --verify are "
            "now unlocked.")
        log("  Next:  fpms-calibrate-lidar --level     (30 seconds, static)")
        log("         fpms-calibrate-lidar --yaw       (the number that matters)")
        return 0
    finally:
        lis.close()


# ===========================================================================
# --yaw   (and the measurement half of --verify)
# ===========================================================================

def one_push(lis, index, total, target_m, window_s):
    """Run one push and return (sample_or_None, reason).

    A rejected push is a rejected push. Nothing here averages a bad sample in
    on the grounds that there is another one coming.
    """
    log("\n  --- push %d of %d ---" % (index, total))
    press_enter("  Line the rover up, press ENTER, then push it STRAIGHT "
                "forward ~%.2f m..." % target_m)
    lis.reset_gyro()
    time.sleep(0.3)
    ref = lis.capture()
    if ref is None:
        return None, "no reference scan"

    res = track_push(lis, ref, target_m, window_s)
    end = res["end"]
    if end is None or end.seq == ref.seq:
        return None, "no new scan arrived during the push - the feed is frozen"

    ok, detail = lis.scan_health()
    if not ok:
        return None, "the scan went bad during the push: %s" % detail

    # The gyro reading for this push, used to de-rotate rather than the scan
    # correlation. See fit_motion() for the 36-push measurement behind this.
    fit = fit_motion(ref, end, chassis_rot_rad=res["gyro"])
    rot, rot_conf = fit["rot"], fit["rot_confidence"]
    dx, dy, rms, inliers = fit["dx"], fit["dy"], fit["rms"], fit["inliers"]
    dist = math.hypot(dx, dy)
    gyro_deg = res["gyro"] * RAD2DEG
    # Reported as the chassis turn, which is what the operator did and what the
    # gyro figure next to it means. The world swept the other way.
    rot_deg = -rot * RAD2DEG

    log("    travelled %.3f m | chassis turned %+.2f deg by scan | gyro %+.2f "
        "deg | icp rms %.3f m over %d points"
        % (dist, rot_deg, gyro_deg, rms, inliers))
    if fit["note"]:
        log("    !! %s" % fit["note"])

    if dist < PUSH_MIN_M:
        return None, ("only %.3f m of travel; below %.2f m the direction is "
                      "dominated by scan noise" % (dist, PUSH_MIN_M))
    if inliers < PUSH_MIN_INLIERS:
        return None, ("ICP kept only %d points; that is not a fit" % inliers)
    if rms > PUSH_MAX_RMS_M:
        return None, ("ICP residual %.3f m is too high - the two scans do not "
                      "describe the same room. Something moved, or the push "
                      "was too long for the overlap." % rms)
    # Gate on the GYRO, not the scan estimate. The scan estimate is confounded
    # by translation and will report several degrees of turn on a dead-straight
    # push, so gating on it both rejects good pushes (6/36 accepted vs 30/36)
    # and, worse, ACCEPTS bad ones: a spurious 4.19 deg sat comfortably inside
    # this threshold while costing 4.28 deg of mount yaw.
    if abs(gyro_deg) > PUSH_TURN_REJECT_DEG:
        return None, ("the gyro says the chassis turned %+.1f deg during the "
                      "push. This measurement is the DIRECTION of apparent "
                      "motion, so a steered push biases it directly."
                      % gyro_deg)

    yaw, conf = sm.yaw_from_straight_push(dx, dy, pushed_forward=True)
    if conf < PUSH_MIN_CONFIDENCE:
        return None, ("the estimator's own confidence is %.2f (needs %.2f); "
                      "push further" % (conf, PUSH_MIN_CONFIDENCE))

    warn = []
    if dist < PUSH_WARN_M:
        warn.append("short push (%.2f m)" % dist)
    if abs(rot_deg) > PUSH_TURN_WARN_DEG:
        warn.append("turned %+.1f deg while pushing" % rot_deg)
    if rot_conf < 0.15:
        warn.append("weak rotation correlation (%.2f) - featureless room?"
                    % rot_conf)
    if abs(rot_deg - gyro_deg) > PUSH_TURN_WARN_DEG:
        warn.append("scan correlation says %+.1f deg of turn, gyro says %+.1f "
                    "- expected on a straight push (the correlation is "
                    "confounded by translation); the gyro was used"
                    % (rot_deg, gyro_deg))
    if fit["note"]:
        warn.append(fit["note"])

    log("    -> mount yaw from this push: %s  (confidence %.2f)%s"
        % (fmt_rad(yaw), conf, ("  [" + "; ".join(warn) + "]") if warn else ""))

    return {
        "yaw_rad": yaw, "yaw_deg": yaw * RAD2DEG, "confidence": round(conf, 3),
        "dist_m": round(dist, 4), "dx": round(dx, 4), "dy": round(dy, 4),
        "scan_turn_deg": round(rot_deg, 3), "gyro_turn_deg": round(gyro_deg, 3),
        "icp_rms_m": round(rms, 4), "icp_inliers": inliers,
        "warnings": warn, "at": stamp(),
    }, ""


def collect_pushes(lis, n_pushes, target_m, window_s):
    """Run n pushes; return (samples, rejects)."""
    samples, rejects = [], []
    attempts = 0
    max_attempts = n_pushes + 3
    while len(samples) < n_pushes and attempts < max_attempts:
        attempts += 1
        s, why = one_push(lis, len(samples) + 1, n_pushes, target_m, window_s)
        if s is None:
            log("    REJECTED: %s" % why)
            rejects.append(why)
            if attempts < max_attempts:
                log("    (that push does not count; do it again)")
            continue
        samples.append(s)
    return samples, rejects


def summarise_yaw(samples):
    """(median_rad, spread_deg, tight, unwrapped_deg). Circular-safe."""
    med_deg, spread, un = circular_median_deg([s["yaw_deg"] for s in samples])
    if med_deg is None:
        return None, None, False, []
    return med_deg * DEG2RAD, spread, spread <= YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG, un


def cmd_yaw(args):
    hr("MOUNT YAW - several straight pushes, by hand")
    state = read_state()
    ok, msg = mirror_gate(state, "--yaw")
    if not ok:
        log("\n" + msg)
        return 1
    if msg:
        log(msg)

    n = args.pushes
    if n < MIN_PUSHES:
        log("\n  refuse: --pushes %d. A median of fewer than %d samples is not "
            "a median, and this number goes into every map the rover ever "
            "builds." % (n, MIN_PUSHES))
        return 1

    log("""
Push the rover STRAIGHT FORWARD, by hand, about %.2f m, %d times. Between
pushes, put it back wherever you like - each push is measured on its own.

WHY THIS WORKS WITHOUT ANY MOTION CALIBRATION: only the DIRECTION of the
apparent motion is used, never its magnitude. counts/mm on this rover is
unmeasured and disputed and it does not enter this measurement at all.

  * straight matters, distance does not. A steered push is rejected outright.
  * push, do not carry. Lifting the rover ends the measurement quietly.
  * a room with structure. A bare corridor has nothing for the matcher to
    hold on to and the tool will tell you so rather than invent a number.""" %
        (args.push_m, n))

    lis = start_listener()
    if lis is None:
        return 2
    try:
        samples, rejects = collect_pushes(lis, n, args.push_m,
                                          args.window or PUSH_WINDOW_S)
    finally:
        lis.close()

    return report_yaw_result(samples, rejects, n, store=True)


def report_yaw_result(samples, rejects, wanted, store):
    hr("RESULT")
    if len(samples) < MIN_PUSHES:
        log("  %d of %d pushes were usable. Refusing to conclude anything from "
            "fewer than %d." % (len(samples), wanted, MIN_PUSHES))
        for r in rejects:
            log("    rejected: %s" % r)
        log("\n  Nothing has been written. Fix whatever the rejections point "
            "at and run --yaw again.")
        return 1

    med_rad, spread, tight, un = summarise_yaw(samples)
    log("  samples (deg): %s" % ", ".join("%+.2f" % v for v in un))
    log("  median       : %s" % fmt_rad(med_rad))
    log("  spread       : %.2f deg peak-to-peak over %d pushes"
        % (spread, len(samples)))
    log("  position cost: %.1f mm of bias at the 0.6 m lever arm the launch "
        "file quotes (1 deg = 10.5 mm)"
        % (abs(med_rad * RAD2DEG) * 10.5))
    if rejects:
        log("  rejected     : %d push(es)" % len(rejects))
        for r in rejects:
            log("      %s" % r)

    if tight:
        log("\n  SPREAD IS TIGHT (<= %.1f deg). The median is trustworthy to "
            "roughly %.2f deg, which is inside the ~0.5 deg the launch file "
            "asks for." % (YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG, spread / 2.0))
    else:
        log("""
  SPREAD IS TOO WIDE (%.2f deg, needs <= %.1f deg). REFUSING TO CONCLUDE.

  The pushes disagree about something real, and a median does not fix that.
  In rough order of likelihood:
    * the mount is loose. Grab the scanner and try to twist it.
    * the pushes were steered. Watch the per-push 'scan turned' figures above.
    * the room has too little structure, or something in it moved between
      pushes (a person counts).
  Fix it and run --yaw again. --apply will not accept this."""
            % (spread, YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG))

    conf = [s["confidence"] for s in samples]
    log("\n  estimator confidence per push: %s"
        % ", ".join("%.2f" % c for c in conf))
    warned = [w for s in samples for w in s["warnings"]]
    if warned:
        log("  caveats carried by accepted pushes:")
        for w in warned:
            log("      %s" % w)

    if store:
        state = read_state()
        state["yaw"] = {
            "median_rad": round(med_rad, 6),
            "median_deg": round(med_rad * RAD2DEG, 4),
            "spread_deg": round(spread, 4),
            "tight": bool(tight),
            "n": len(samples), "rejected": len(rejects),
            "samples": samples,
            "at": stamp(),
        }
        save_state(state)
        log("\n  recorded in %s" % STATE_FILE)
        if tight:
            log("  Next:  fpms-calibrate-lidar --lever    (x/y cross-check)")
            log("         fpms-calibrate-lidar --apply    (writes config.env)")
    return 0 if tight else 1


# ===========================================================================
# --lever
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_lever(args):
    hr("LEVER ARM - where the scanner sits, weakly, from a turn in place")
    state = read_state()
    ok, msg = mirror_gate(state, "--lever")
    if not ok:
        log("\n" + msg)
        return 1
    if msg:
        log(msg)

    log("""
Turning the chassis about base_link's origin swings an off-centre scanner
through an arc, and the size of that arc is the lever arm. Read the caveat
before you believe the number:

  * IT ASSUMES THE ROTATION WAS ABOUT base_link's ORIGIN. A hand-turned rover
    pivots wherever it happens to pivot, and that is exactly the assumption
    most likely to be false here.
  * x and y are only PARTLY observable this way. The estimator's own docstring
    calls this weakly observed and says to treat it as a sanity check on a
    ruler, not a replacement for one.
  * z is not observable AT ALL from a 2D scan. Measure it with a rule.

So: turn the rover on the spot ~%.0f degrees, about its own centre, as well as
you can by hand.""" % MIRROR_TARGET_DEG)

    lis = start_listener()
    if lis is None:
        return 2
    try:
        press_enter("\n  Press ENTER, then turn the rover on the spot...")
        lis.reset_gyro()
        time.sleep(0.3)
        ref = lis.capture()
        if ref is None:
            log("  no reference scan; aborting")
            return 2
        res = track_turn(lis, ref, MIRROR_TARGET_DEG * DEG2RAD,
                         args.window or MIRROR_WINDOW_S)
        end = res["end"]
        if end is None or end.seq == ref.seq:
            log("\n  REFUSING: no new scan during the turn; the feed is frozen.")
            return 1
        ok, detail = lis.scan_health()
        if not ok:
            log("\n  REFUSING: %s" % detail)
            return 1

        fit = fit_motion(ref, end)
        rot, rot_conf = fit["rot"], fit["rot_confidence"]
        dx, dy, rms, inliers = fit["dx"], fit["dy"], fit["rms"], fit["inliers"]
        log("\n  turned      : %+.1f deg by scan, %+.1f deg by gyro "
            "(correlation %.2f)"
            % (-rot * RAD2DEG, res["gyro"] * RAD2DEG, rot_conf))
        log("  apparent dt : dx=%+.4f m dy=%+.4f m (icp rms %.3f m, %d points)"
            % (dx, dy, rms, inliers))
        if fit["note"]:
            log("  !! %s" % fit["note"])

        if inliers < PUSH_MIN_INLIERS or rms > PUSH_MAX_RMS_M:
            log("\n  REFUSING: the scan match is not good enough to derive a "
                "lever arm from (rms %.3f m over %d points)." % (rms, inliers))
            return 1

        lx, ly, err, detail = lever_arm(dx, dy, rot)
        if err:
            log("\n  no lever arm: %s" % err)
            return 1
        log("  %s" % detail)

        log("\n  scanner offset in base_link, ESTIMATED:")
        log("      laser_x ~ %+.3f m   (%+.0f mm)" % (lx, lx * 1000.0))
        log("      laser_y ~ %+.3f m   (%+.0f mm)" % (ly, ly * 1000.0))
        log("""
  CONFIDENCE: LOW, and structurally so - not because this run went badly.
  The solve assumes the pivot was base_link's origin. If you actually turned
  the rover about a point 50 mm off that origin, this answer is wrong by
  roughly 50 mm and nothing in the data says so.

  USE IT AS A CROSS-CHECK. Put a rule on the robot: horizontal distance from
  the drive axle centreline to the LiDAR's OPTICAL CENTRE (the rotating mirror
  axis, not the case edge), +x out the nose, +y to the LEFT. If the rule and
  the estimate above disagree by more than a couple of centimetres, believe
  the rule and distrust the turn.""")

        state = read_state()
        state["lever"] = {
            "lx": round(lx, 4), "ly": round(ly, 4),
            "rotation_deg": round(-rot * RAD2DEG, 2),
            "gyro_deg": round(res["gyro"] * RAD2DEG, 2),
            "icp_rms_m": round(rms, 4), "icp_inliers": inliers,
            "caveat": "weakly observed; assumes rotation about base_link origin",
            "at": stamp(),
        }
        save_state(state)
        return 0
    finally:
        lis.close()


# ===========================================================================
# --level
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_level(args):
    hr("LEVEL - is the scan plane striking the floor?")
    log("""
Park the rover somewhere typical and leave it alone. This reads ONE static
scan and looks for the signature of a tilted scan plane: a band of short,
consistent returns clustered in bearing, which is what the beam grazing the
floor looks like from above.

It DIAGNOSES. It never estimates an angle - a 2D scan carries no height
information, so roll and pitch cannot be solved from it. What it can do is
tell you whether to go and look, and the arithmetic behind why that matters:
at 0.10 m above the floor, 1 degree of downward pitch puts the floor strike at
5.7 m and 2 degrees puts it at 2.9 m. This room returns a median near 2 m, so
1-2 degrees turns the far half of every scan into a ring of phantom obstacles
that moves with the robot.""")

    lis = start_listener()
    if lis is None:
        return 2
    try:
        # Several scans, not one: the freshness check needs to see the feed
        # change, and one scan cannot show that.
        time.sleep(1.5)
        ok, detail = lis.scan_health()
        log("\n  %s" % detail)
        if not ok:
            log("  REFUSING: %s" % detail)
            return 1
        s = lis.capture()
        out = sm.plane_level_diagnostic(s.ranges, s.angle_min,
                                        s.angle_increment)
    finally:
        lis.close()

    log("\n  short-return fraction : %.1f%%" % (100.0 * out["short_frac"]))
    log("  %s" % out["detail"])
    if out["floor_strike_suspected"]:
        log("""
  FLOOR STRIKE SUSPECTED.

  Do not try to correct this in laser_pitch. A tilted plane still slices the
  room at an angle; the TF only places the returns, it cannot un-tilt them.
  Level the mount mechanically - a small spirit level or a phone inclinometer
  on the LiDAR's mounting face, target under 0.5 degrees - and re-run this.
  If you cannot get it level, measure the residual and set laser_roll/pitch so
  the returns at least land in the right place.""")
    else:
        log("\n  No floor strike apparent. That is not proof the mount is "
            "level - a clutter-free room hides a small tilt - but nothing here "
            "argues against it.")

    log("\n  STILL NOT MEASURED BY ANY OF THIS: laser_z. A 2D scan has no "
        "height information at all.")
    log("  Measure it with a rule: (height of the optical centre above the "
        "floor) - 0.035 m, because base_link sits at half the measured 70 mm "
        "wheel diameter.")

    state = read_state()
    state["level"] = {
        "floor_strike_suspected": bool(out["floor_strike_suspected"]),
        "short_frac": round(float(out["short_frac"]), 4),
        "detail": out["detail"], "at": stamp(),
    }
    save_state(state)
    return 0


# ===========================================================================
# --verify
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_verify(args):
    hr("VERIFY - after applying, the residual mount yaw must read ~0")
    state = read_state()
    ok, msg = mirror_gate(state, "--verify")
    if not ok:
        log("\n" + msg)
        return 1
    if msg:
        log(msg)

    cfg = read_config()
    applied = config_float(cfg, "FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW")
    measured_flag = cfg.get(MEASURED_KEY, "0")
    if applied is None:
        log("\n  refuse: FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW is absent or unparseable in %s. "
            "There is nothing applied to verify." % CONFIG)
        return 1

    log("""
Applying FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW changes the TF TREE. It does not change one byte of
%s - the scan arrives in the laser frame either way. So this does NOT expect
the raw measurement to have moved to zero. It re-measures the mount yaw from
fresh pushes and subtracts what is actually applied:

    residual = measured - applied      must read ~0 (within %.1f deg)

A SIGN ERROR does not make that residual merely wrong. It makes it almost
exactly twice the applied value, which is a signature this can name rather
than a mismatch it can only report.

  applied in %s : %s
  %s : %s
""" % (SCAN_TOPIC, VERIFY_RESIDUAL_PASS_DEG, CONFIG, fmt_rad(applied),
       MEASURED_KEY, measured_flag))

    if measured_flag != "1":
        log("  NOTE: %s is %r, so fpms-tf is running with measured:=%s and "
            "Nav2/SLAM still refuse to start. Verifying anyway - the TF value "
            "is what it is." % (MEASURED_KEY, measured_flag, measured_flag))

    n = max(MIN_PUSHES, args.pushes)
    lis = start_listener(want_tf=True)
    if lis is None:
        return 2
    tf_live = None
    try:
        # Give latched /tf_static a moment; it is TRANSIENT_LOCAL so it should
        # arrive on subscription, but only if fpms-tf is actually running.
        if lis.tf_available:
            deadline = time.monotonic() + 3.0
            while time.monotonic() < deadline:
                tf_live = lis.tf_lookup(BASE_LINK, LASER_FRAME)
                if tf_live:
                    break
                time.sleep(0.2)
        samples, rejects = collect_pushes(lis, n, args.push_m,
                                          args.window or PUSH_WINDOW_S)
    finally:
        lis.close()

    hr("VERIFY RESULT")
    if len(samples) < MIN_PUSHES:
        log("  only %d usable pushes; refusing to verify anything from fewer "
            "than %d." % (len(samples), MIN_PUSHES))
        for r in rejects:
            log("    rejected: %s" % r)
        return 1

    med_rad, spread, tight, un = summarise_yaw(samples)
    residual = math.radians(wrap_deg((med_rad - applied) * RAD2DEG))
    res_deg = residual * RAD2DEG

    log("  samples (deg)  : %s" % ", ".join("%+.2f" % v for v in un))
    log("  measured yaw   : %s  (spread %.2f deg over %d pushes)"
        % (fmt_rad(med_rad), spread, len(samples)))
    log("  applied yaw    : %s" % fmt_rad(applied))
    log("  RESIDUAL       : %s" % fmt_rad(residual))
    log("  position cost  : %.1f mm at the 0.6 m lever arm"
        % (abs(res_deg) * 10.5))

    # --- the live TF, not just the file ------------------------------------
    log("")
    if tf_live:
        log("  live %s -> %s from %s:" % (BASE_LINK, LASER_FRAME,
                                          TF_STATIC_TOPIC))
        log("      x=%+.4f y=%+.4f z=%+.4f yaw=%s"
            % (tf_live["x"], tf_live["y"], tf_live["z"],
               fmt_rad(tf_live["yaw"])))
        drift = abs(wrap_deg((tf_live["yaw"] - applied) * RAD2DEG))
        if drift > VERIFY_TF_MATCH_DEG:
            log("      MISMATCH: what is BROADCAST differs from %s by %.2f deg."
                % (CONFIG, drift))
            log("      That is the 'edited config.env, never restarted the "
                "unit' failure. Fix it:  sudo systemctl restart fpms-tf")
        else:
            log("      matches config.env - fpms-tf is running the value you "
                "think it is.")
    elif lis.tf_available:
        log("  NOTE: nothing arrived on %s within 3 s, so the LIVE transform "
            "could not be checked - only the number in %s."
            % (TF_STATIC_TOPIC, CONFIG))
        log("        Either fpms-tf is not running (systemctl status fpms-tf), "
            "or DDS is on shared memory again (see FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_"
            "FILE). An empty /tf_static with a live publisher is a known "
            "signature of the latter.")

    # --- the verdict --------------------------------------------------------
    log("")
    if not tight:
        log("  INCONCLUSIVE: this run's own spread is %.2f deg (needs <= %.1f). "
            "The residual cannot be trusted to better than that."
            % (spread, YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG))
        verdict = "inconclusive"
        rc = 1
    elif abs(res_deg) <= VERIFY_RESIDUAL_PASS_DEG:
        log("  PASS. The residual mount yaw is %+.2f deg, inside +/-%.1f. The "
            "applied transform is correct in magnitude AND in sign."
            % (res_deg, VERIFY_RESIDUAL_PASS_DEG))
        verdict = "pass"
        rc = 0
    else:
        verdict = "fail"
        rc = 1
        log("  FAIL. The residual is %+.2f deg - %.1f mm of bias on every fix."
            % (res_deg, abs(res_deg) * 10.5))
        # The sign-error signature: residual ~= 2 * measured, i.e. applied is
        # about the negative of what was measured.
        if abs(applied) > 0.5 * DEG2RAD and \
                abs(wrap_deg((med_rad + applied) * RAD2DEG)) < \
                max(1.0, 0.25 * abs(res_deg)):
            log("""
  THIS IS A SIGN ERROR, not a bad measurement.

  The residual is almost exactly twice the measured yaw, which is what you get
  when the correct magnitude is applied with the wrong sign. What is applied is
  %s; what the rover is telling you it should be is %s.

  Fix it and re-verify:
      FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW=%.6f      in %s
      sudo systemctl restart fpms-tf
      fpms-calibrate-lidar --verify""" % (fmt_rad(applied), fmt_rad(med_rad),
                                          med_rad, CONFIG))
        else:
            log("""
  Possible causes, in order:
    * the mount moved since --yaw was run. Grab the scanner and twist.
    * laser_yaw was hand-edited to something other than what --apply wrote.
    * the correction is being applied TWICE. LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG in
      fpms_lidar_ros.py rotates the scan in the same sense laser_yaw does;
      correct mount yaw in exactly ONE of the two places. --report shows what
      that constant currently is.
  Set FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW=%.6f, restart fpms-tf, and verify again.""" % med_rad)

    state = read_state()
    state["verify"] = {
        "verdict": verdict,
        "applied_rad": round(applied, 6),
        "measured_rad": round(med_rad, 6),
        "residual_deg": round(res_deg, 4),
        "spread_deg": round(spread, 4), "n": len(samples),
        "tf_static_yaw_rad": (round(tf_live["yaw"], 6) if tf_live else None),
        "at": stamp(),
    }
    save_state(state)

    log("""
  THE ONE CHECK THIS PROGRAM CANNOT DO FOR YOU
  --------------------------------------------
  Everything above is self-consistent arithmetic. If the whole stack agreed on
  a mirrored idea of which way is positive, every number here would agree with
  every other and all of them would be wrong together. Close that loop with
  your eyes, once:

      park the rover with a flat wall ~1 m dead ahead
      rviz2 -> Fixed Frame: base_link, add LaserScan on %s
      the wall must appear IN FRONT of the robot, square to it, at ~1 m

  If it appears rotated, the sign convention is wrong somewhere upstream of
  everything this tool measures.""" % SCAN_TOPIC)
    return rc


# ===========================================================================
# --report
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_report(args):
    hr("WHAT IS SET, AND WHERE EACH NUMBER CAME FROM")
    cfg = read_config()
    state = read_state()

    log("\nconfig.env: %s" % CONFIG)
    if not cfg:
        log("  (unreadable or empty - every service falls back to defaults "
            "and retries forever, with no error anywhere)")
    for k in LASER_KEYS:
        log("  %-22s = %s" % (k, cfg.get(k, "<unset>")))
    log("  %-22s = %s" % (MEASURED_KEY, cfg.get(MEASURED_KEY, "<unset>")))
    if cfg.get(MEASURED_KEY) != "1":
        log("\n  %s is not 1, so:" % MEASURED_KEY)
        log("     fpms-nav2.service and both SLAM units REFUSE TO START;")
        log("     fpms-tf publishes whatever is above with measured:=0;")
        log("     fpms-selftest reports the mount transform as unmeasured.")

    path, sign, zero = read_lidar_ros_constants()
    log("\nfpms_lidar_ros.py: %s" % (path or "not found"))
    log("  LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN   = %s" % ("%+d" % sign if sign is not None
                                          else "unknown"))
    log("  LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG = %s" % (zero if zero is not None
                                          else "unknown"))
    if zero not in (None, 0.0):
        log("  !! NON-ZERO. That constant rotates the scan in the SAME sense "
            "as laser_yaw. Mount yaw must be corrected in exactly ONE of the "
            "two places; applying half in each is how you end up chasing a "
            "bias that moves whenever either file is touched.")

    log("\nmeasurement state: %s" % STATE_FILE)
    if not state:
        log("  (nothing measured yet - start with --mirror-check)")
    else:
        m = state.get("mirror")
        if m:
            d = age_days(m.get("at"))
            log("  mirror   : %s  (%s%s)"
                % (m.get("verdict"), m.get("at"),
                   ", %.0f days ago" % d if d is not None else ""))
            log("             scan %+.1f deg vs gyro %+.1f deg, taken with "
                "LIDAR_ROTATION_SIGN=%s"
                % (m.get("scan_deg", 0.0), m.get("gyro_deg", 0.0),
                   m.get("rotation_sign")))
            log("             %s" % m.get("detail", ""))
        else:
            log("  mirror   : never run. Nothing else may run until it passes.")

        y = state.get("yaw")
        if y:
            log("  yaw      : %s from %d pushes, spread %.2f deg (%s)  [%s]"
                % (fmt_rad(y["median_rad"]), y.get("n", 0),
                   y.get("spread_deg", 0.0),
                   "TIGHT" if y.get("tight") else "TOO WIDE - not usable",
                   y.get("at")))
            for s in y.get("samples", []):
                log("             push %.3f m -> %+.2f deg (conf %.2f, "
                    "turn %+.2f deg, icp rms %.3f m)"
                    % (s.get("dist_m", 0.0), s.get("yaw_deg", 0.0),
                       s.get("confidence", 0.0), s.get("scan_turn_deg", 0.0),
                       s.get("icp_rms_m", 0.0)))
        else:
            log("  yaw      : not measured")

        lv = state.get("lever")
        if lv:
            log("  lever    : x ~ %+.3f m, y ~ %+.3f m from a %+.1f deg turn "
                "[%s]" % (lv["lx"], lv["ly"], lv.get("rotation_deg", 0.0),
                          lv.get("at")))
            log("             WEAKLY OBSERVED - %s" % lv.get("caveat", ""))
        else:
            log("  lever    : not measured")

        lvl = state.get("level")
        if lvl:
            log("  level    : %s [%s]"
                % ("FLOOR STRIKE SUSPECTED" if lvl["floor_strike_suspected"]
                   else "no floor strike apparent", lvl.get("at")))
        else:
            log("  level    : not checked")

        v = state.get("verify")
        if v:
            log("  verify   : %s, residual %+.2f deg against applied %s [%s]"
                % (v.get("verdict", "?").upper(), v.get("residual_deg", 0.0),
                   fmt_rad(v.get("applied_rad", 0.0)), v.get("at")))
        else:
            log("  verify   : never run - the applied value has not been "
                "checked against the rover")

    log("\nestimator: %s" % LIB_DIR)
    try:
        log("  %s" % convention_detail())
    except SystemExit as exc:
        log("  CONVENTION PROBE FAILED: %s" % exc)
        log("  No measuring subcommand will run until that is explained.")

    log("""
NOT MEASURABLE BY THIS TOOL, EVER
  laser_z       a 2D scan carries no height information. Use a rule:
                (optical centre above the floor) - 0.035 m.
  laser_roll    only diagnosable as a floor strike (--level), never solvable.
  laser_pitch   same. Level the mount mechanically; target under 0.5 deg.
  laser_x/y     partly observable (--lever) and weakly. Believe a rule over it.""")
    return 0


# ===========================================================================
# --apply
# ===========================================================================

def cmd_apply(args):
    hr("APPLY - write the measured transform into config.env")
    state = read_state()

    # ---- gate 1: the mirror -------------------------------------------------
    ok, msg = mirror_gate(state, "--apply")
    if not ok:
        log("\n" + msg)
        return 1
    if msg:
        log(msg)

    # ---- gate 2: a tight yaw measurement -----------------------------------
    y = state.get("yaw")
    if not y:
        log("\n  refuse: mount yaw has never been measured. Run "
            "fpms-calibrate-lidar --yaw first.")
        return 1
    if not y.get("tight"):
        log("\n  refuse: the last --yaw run had a spread of %.2f deg (needs "
            "<= %.1f). Writing a median of samples that disagree by that much "
            "would publish a number nobody has any right to trust."
            % (y.get("spread_deg", 999.0), YAW_SPREAD_TIGHT_DEG))
        return 1
    if y.get("n", 0) < MIN_PUSHES:
        log("\n  refuse: only %d pushes are on record; %d is the minimum."
            % (y.get("n", 0), MIN_PUSHES))
        return 1
    d = age_days(y.get("at"))
    if d is not None and d > YAW_STALE_WARN_DAYS:
        log("\n  note: the yaw measurement is %.0f days old. If the rover has "
            "been knocked about since, re-run --yaw." % d)

    # ---- gate 3: the double-correction trap --------------------------------
    path, sign, zero = read_lidar_ros_constants()
    if zero is None:
        log("\n  note: could not read LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG (looked in %s). "
            "Check by hand that it is still 0.0 - it rotates the scan in the "
            "same sense as laser_yaw." % (", ".join(LIDAR_ROS_CANDIDATES),))
    elif zero != 0.0:
        log("""
  refuse: LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG is %s in %s.

  That constant rotates the scan in the SAME SENSE as laser_yaw. Writing a
  mount yaw here as well applies the correction TWICE, which lands somewhere
  that looks almost right and moves whenever either file is touched.

  Correct mount yaw in exactly ONE place, and the launch file says which one:
  here, in laser_yaw. Set LIDAR_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG back to 0.0, restart
  fpms-lidar-ros, re-run --mirror-check and --yaw (the measurement was taken
  under the old value), then apply.""" % (zero, path))
        return 1

    # ---- gate 4: a terminal and a human ------------------------------------
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        log("\n  refuse: --apply needs a terminal. It must never run from a "
            "script, a unit file or a cron job: the whole point of this value "
            "is that a person looked at the evidence and accepted it.")
        return 1

    med_rad = float(y["median_rad"])
    cfg = read_config()
    lvl = state.get("level") or {}
    lever = state.get("lever") or {}

    log("\n  EVIDENCE")
    log("  --------")
    log("  mirror : %s (%s)" % (state["mirror"]["verdict"],
                                state["mirror"].get("at")))
    log("  yaw    : %s, median of %d pushes, spread %.2f deg (%s)"
        % (fmt_rad(med_rad), y["n"], y["spread_deg"], y.get("at")))
    for s in y.get("samples", []):
        log("             %.3f m push -> %+.2f deg (confidence %.2f)"
            % (s.get("dist_m", 0.0), s.get("yaw_deg", 0.0),
               s.get("confidence", 0.0)))
    if lvl:
        log("  level  : %s" % ("FLOOR STRIKE SUSPECTED - %s" % lvl.get("detail")
                               if lvl.get("floor_strike_suspected")
                               else "no floor strike apparent"))
    else:
        log("  level  : NOT CHECKED. Run --level; a tilted scan plane turns "
            "the far half of every scan into phantom floor obstacles, and no "
            "TF value fixes that.")
    if lever:
        log("  lever  : x ~ %+.3f m, y ~ %+.3f m (WEAK - %s)"
            % (lever["lx"], lever["ly"], lever.get("caveat", "")))

    if lvl.get("floor_strike_suspected"):
        log("""
  WARNING: the last --level run suspected a floor strike. Declaring the mount
  MEASURED unblocks Nav2 and SLAM, and they will then map a room that contains
  a ring of phantom obstacles which moves with the robot. Level the mount
  first unless you know better.""")

    # ---- the translation, from a rule --------------------------------------
    log("""
  TRANSLATION - FROM A RULE, NOT FROM THIS TOOL
  ---------------------------------------------
  x and y are only weakly observable from a turn and z is not observable at
  all. Measure them: horizontal distance from base_link (chassis centreline,
  over the drive axle, 35 mm above the floor) to the LiDAR's OPTICAL CENTRE -
  the rotating mirror axis, not the case edge. +x out the nose, +y to the LEFT,
  +z up from base_link, i.e. (height above floor) - 0.035.

  Press ENTER at any prompt to leave that value as it is.""")

    updates = {}
    prompts = (
        ("FPMS_TF_LASER_X", "laser_x (m, +forward)",
         lever.get("lx")),
        ("FPMS_TF_LASER_Y", "laser_y (m, +left)",
         lever.get("ly")),
        ("FPMS_TF_LASER_Z", "laser_z (m, +up from base_link)", None),
    )
    for key, label, hint in prompts:
        cur = cfg.get(key, "<unset>")
        extra = ""
        if hint is not None:
            extra = "  [turn-test estimate %+.3f, weak]" % hint
        raw = ask("    %s  currently %s%s: " % (label, cur, extra))
        if raw == "":
            continue
        try:
            val = float(raw)
        except ValueError:
            log("      not a number; leaving %s unchanged" % key)
            continue
        if abs(val) > 1.0:
            log("      refuse: %.3f m. This chassis is not a metre across - "
                "that looks like millimetres typed into a metres field. "
                "Leaving %s unchanged." % (val, key))
            continue
        if hint is not None and abs(val - hint) > 0.03:
            log("      note: the rule and the turn-test disagree by %.0f mm. "
                "Believing the rule, as documented - but if you did not "
                "actually measure it, stop and measure it."
                % (abs(val - hint) * 1000.0))
        updates[key] = "%.4f" % val

    updates["FPMS_TF_LASER_YAW"] = "%.6f" % med_rad
    updates[MEASURED_KEY] = "1"

    log("\n  ABOUT TO WRITE to %s" % CONFIG)
    for k in sorted(updates):
        log("      %-24s %s  ->  %s" % (k, cfg.get(k, "<unset>"), updates[k]))
    log("\n  FPMS_TF_LASER_ROLL and FPMS_TF_LASER_PITCH are NOT written. They "
        "are not solvable from a 2D scan; level the mount and, if you have a "
        "residual off an inclinometer, set them by hand in radians.")
    log("""
  Setting %s=1 unblocks fpms-nav2.service and both SLAM units, which refuse to
  start while it is 0. Everything they produce from here on inherits these
  numbers.""" % MEASURED_KEY)

    answer = ask("\n  Type  yes  to write this, anything else to abort: ")
    if answer.lower() != "yes":
        log("\n  aborted; %s is untouched" % CONFIG)
        return 1

    ok, detail = write_config_values(updates)
    if not ok:
        log("\n  NOT WRITTEN: %s" % detail)
        return 1
    log("\n  %s" % detail)

    st = read_state()
    st["applied"] = {"values": updates, "at": stamp(),
                     "from": "median of %d hand pushes, spread %.2f deg"
                             % (y["n"], y["spread_deg"])}
    save_state(st)

    log("""
  NOW DO THESE, IN THIS ORDER
  ---------------------------
      sudo systemctl restart fpms-tf
      fpms-calibrate-lidar --verify

  The restart is not optional: systemd read those values into the unit's
  environment at start, so until fpms-tf restarts it is still broadcasting the
  old transform while config.env claims otherwise. --verify reads the LIVE
  transform off /tf_static and will catch exactly that.

  --verify is also how a SIGN error gets caught, and sign errors are this
  project's signature failure. Do not skip it because the number looks right.""")
    return 0


# ===========================================================================
# MAIN
# ===========================================================================

def build_parser():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="fpms-calibrate-lidar",
        description="Measure base_link -> laser_frame by hand. Publishes to "
                    "nothing and cannot move the rover.",
        epilog="Order: --mirror-check, --level, --yaw, --lever, --apply, "
               "--verify. Everything but --mirror-check, --level and --report "
               "refuses to run until the mirror check passes.")
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    g.add_argument("--mirror-check", action="store_true",
                   help="turn ~90 deg by hand: is the scan mirrored? DO THIS "
                        "FIRST - everything else refuses until it passes")
    g.add_argument("--yaw", action="store_true",
                   help="several straight hand pushes: the mount yaw, with "
                        "median and spread")
    g.add_argument("--lever", action="store_true",
                   help="turn in place: laser x/y, weakly observed")
    g.add_argument("--level", action="store_true",
                   help="static scan: is the scan plane striking the floor?")
    g.add_argument("--verify", action="store_true",
                   help="re-measure and assert the residual now reads ~0. THE "
                        "important one - it is how a sign error is caught")
    g.add_argument("--report", action="store_true",
                   help="what is set, and where each number came from")
    g.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true",
                   help="write the measured values into config.env, after "
                        "gates and an interactive confirmation")
    ap.add_argument("--pushes", type=int, default=3, metavar="N",
                    help="pushes for --yaw / --verify (default 3, minimum %d)"
                         % MIN_PUSHES)
    ap.add_argument("--push-m", type=float, default=PUSH_TARGET_M,
                    metavar="M",
                    help="how far to push each time (default %.2f m)"
                         % PUSH_TARGET_M)
    ap.add_argument("--window", type=float, default=None, metavar="SEC",
                    help="how long each hand movement may take before the "
                         "tool stops watching (default %.0f s)" % PUSH_WINDOW_S)
    return ap


def main(argv=None):
    ap = build_parser()
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if args.report:
        return cmd_report(args)
    if args.mirror_check:
        return cmd_mirror_check(args)
    if args.level:
        return cmd_level(args)
    if args.yaw:
        return cmd_yaw(args)
    if args.lever:
        return cmd_lever(args)
    if args.verify:
        return cmd_verify(args)
    if args.apply:
        return cmd_apply(args)

    ap.print_help()
    log("""
Start here:

    source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
    fpms-calibrate-lidar --mirror-check

Nothing in this program publishes to any topic, so it cannot move the rover and
needs no arming. You move it; it watches.""")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        log("\naborted; nothing written")
        sys.exit(1)
